00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Did the write do what you meant? Update operators, matchedCount, and modifiedCount

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,300
Ticket 1001 is open, assigned to 201, with two embedded events.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,100
The filter names the ticket AND the state we expect it to be in.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,500
Both hold, so the document matches.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,500
$set changed two fields, $push appended event 5099: one document modified.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,300
All of it is ONE single-document write. No reader sees the new status without its event.

7
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,800
Ticket 1001 is no longer "open", so nothing matches: 0 and 0.

8
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:37,300
No duplicate 5099. That's the expected-state filter working, not a connection failure.

9
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,300
Found the document, but it already holds that value: matched 1, modified 0.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,800
modifiedCount 0 alone can't tell you which case happened. Read both, then read the document.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,900
A replacement document becomes the ENTIRE body (the _id is kept).

12
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,900
Every omitted field, including the event history, would be gone.

13
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,900
Choose updateOne with operators on purpose, and verify by reading the final document.

14
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:06,500
Remember: Put the expected current state in the update filter. $set changes named fields; $push appends to an array. One updateOne on one document is atomic: no half-written ticket. matched 1 / modified 0 = found, value already there. 0 / 0 = no match. replaceOne swaps the whole body. Read the final document.

